    id: 'aimah_ahle_bait_17B',
    title: 'The Grandson of the Prophet (PBUH), Imam Hasan al-Mujtaba (AS)',
    date: '01-01-2015',
    category: ['aimah_ahle_bait'],
    excerpt: 'The Grandson of the Prophet (PBUH), Imam Hasan al-Mujtaba (AS)',
    image: 'https://res.cloudinary.com/dlafcjt6z/image/upload/v1783929558/columns_fpkl28.png',
    content: `
      <p><strong>The Grandson of the Prophet (PBUH), Imam Hasan al-Mujtaba (AS)</strong></p>
      <p><strong>By: Shabbir Ahmed Shigri</strong></p>
      <p>It is an established fact that Imam Hasan (AS) was the grandson of the Prophet of Islam. The Quran has granted him the status of the son of the Prophet (PBUH) and has given a place to his mention in its verses. The Master of both worlds (PBUH) himself narrated countless traditions regarding him. In one tradition, the Prophet (PBUH) said: "I love Hasan and Hussain, and I look with esteem upon those who love them." A companion states: "I saw the Prophet (PBUH) carrying Imam Hasan on one shoulder and Imam Hussain on the other, kissing both of them one after the other." Another companion narrates: "One day, the Prophet (PBUH) was praying, and Hasan and Hussain climbed onto his back. Someone tried to stop them, but the Prophet (PBUH) signaled them to desist." Another companion says: "I began to love Imam Hasan immensely from the day I saw the Prophet (PBUH) sitting with him in his lap and playing with him in the valley." One day, the Master of the Universe (PBUH) was carrying Imam Hasan on his shoulder. A companion said: "O son! How good your ride is!" Hearing this, the Prophet (PBUH) said: "And what a good rider!" (Usd al-Ghabah, vol. 3, p. 51). Referencing Tirmidhi and Imam Muslim, they write: "One day, the Prophet (PBUH), with Imam Hasan on his shoulder, said: 'God loves him, so you too should love him.'" Hafiz Abu Nu'aim narrates from Abu Burdah: "Once, the Prophet (PBUH) was leading congregational prayer when suddenly Imam Hasan came and ran and sat on the back of the Prophet (PBUH). Seeing this, the Prophet (PBUH) gently raised his head. At the end of the prayer, when he was mentioned, he said: 'This son of mine is a Sayyid (master), and look, he will soon reconcile two large groups.'" Imam Nasa'i narrates from Abdullah ibn Shaddad: "One day, the Prophet (PBUH) came to lead the Isha prayer. Imam Hasan was in his lap. The Prophet (PBUH) began the prayer, and when he went into prostration, he remained in it for so long that I thought perhaps revelation was descending upon him. At the end of the prayer, when it was mentioned to him, he said: 'My son had climbed onto my back, and I did not want to remove him from my back until he descended himself.'" Hakim Tirmidhi, Nasa'i, and Abu Dawud have written: "One day, the Prophet (PBUH) was delivering a sermon when the feet of Imam Hasan and Imam Hussain got entangled in the hem of his cloak, and they fell to the ground. Seeing this, the Prophet (PBUH) abandoned the sermon, descended from the pulpit, lifted them into his lap, and went up to the pulpit and began the sermon, saying: 'The leadership of Hasan is among the established facts of the people of Paradise.'" The scholars of Islam agree that the Master of the Universe (PBUH) said: "Al-Hasan and al-Husain are the masters of the youth of Paradise, and their father Ali ibn Abi Talib (AS) is better than them." It is narrated in Bayan al-Kafi: "One day, I saw the Prophet (PBUH) extremely delighted and asked: 'Master! What is the cause of this excessive joy today?' He said: 'Today Gabriel (AS) gave me the glad tidings that my two sons, Hasan and Hussain, are the masters of the youth of Paradise, and their father, Ali ibn Abi Talib (AS), is better than them.'" This Hadith also clarifies that the Prophet (PBUH) was not only a master of honor but also the father of the sons of honor. Historians say: "One day, Abu Sufyan came to Ali (AS) and said: 'Please intercede with the Prophet (PBUH) to write an agreement by which I can achieve my goal.' Ali (AS) said: 'The Prophet (PBUH) is not here.' So he asked for intercession from Imam Hasan (AS), but he was only 14 months old at the time. However, at that moment, he gave such a display of courage that is recorded on the tongue of history. It is written that he grabbed Abu Sufyan by the collar and twisted his nose, saying: 'This testimony on the tongue is everything for our denial.' Seeing this, the Commander of the Faithful (AS) was pleased." Allamah Majlisi writes: "It was the practice of Imam Hasan (AS) that, even at a very young age, he would narrate, word for word, the revelation descending upon his grandfather to his noble mother." One day, Ali (AS) said: "O daughter of the Prophet (PBUH)! I wish to see and hear Hasan reciting the divine revelation myself." And the Lady described the incident behind Imam Hasan. One day, the Commander of the Faithful (AS) entered the house before Hasan (AS) and hid in a corner. Imam Hasan (AS) arrived as usual and sat in his mother's lap and began to recite, but after a while, he said: "O Mother! Today, the flow of my tongue is hindered in expressing the purpose. I feel as if my respected elder is watching me." Hearing this, the Commander of the Faithful (AS) came out of the room, took Hasan (AS) into his lap, and began to kiss him." Imam Zayn al-Abidin (AS) says: "Imam Hasan (AS) was a great worshipper, an unmatched ascetic, and the most superior scholar. Whenever he performed Hajj, he did so on foot. He never performed Hajj while riding. He frequently remembered death, punishment, the grave, the Bridge (Sirat), and the gathering and resurrection and wept. When he performed ablution, his face would turn yellow. When he stood for prayer, he would tremble like a willow branch. Upon reaching the door of the house, he would address God, saying: 'My God! Your sinful servant has come to Your threshold. O Merciful One! Forgive me, the evil-doer, by the sake of Your goodness.' He would then remain seated silently until the sun rose." Imam Shafi'i (RA) writes: "Imam Hasan (AS) often distributed all his wealth in the path of God. Sometimes, he would give away half of his wealth. He was extremely generous." Historians write: "A person started asking Imam Hasan (AS) for something. His questions were very long. He gave him 50,000 dirhams and five hundred gold coins and said: 'Bring [your request] every time and hand it over.' After that, he offered his own cloak as wages for every time. Once, he saw a beggar in need. He gave him ten thousand dirhams and sent the amount to his home." Someone asked him: "You often face poverty yourself, but you do not turn away a beggar." He replied: "I am a beggar of God, so I have developed the habit of giving. I fear that if I change my habit, God might also change His habit and deprive me as well." Regarding Tawakkul (trust), Imam Shafi'i (RA) narrates: "Someone said to Imam Hasan (AS): 'Abu Dharr al-Ghifari used to say: I prefer poverty over wealth and illness over health.' He replied: 'May God have mercy on Abu Dharr. His statement is correct, but I say: Whoever trusts in God's decree and destiny will always like what God likes for him.'" In the field of knowledge and ethics, Allamah Ibn Shahr Ashub writes: "One day, Imam Hasan (AS) was riding a horse somewhere when an opponent appeared on the path and started cursing him. He did not reply at all. When the opponent was done, he approached him, greeted him, and said: 'Brother, perhaps you are a traveler. If you are hungry, I can feed you. If you need clothes, I can give you clothes. If you need a place to stay, I can arrange accommodation. If you need wealth, I can give you enough to become prosperous.'"</p>
    `
