    image: 'https://res.cloudinary.com/dlafcjt6z/image/upload/v1783929558/columns_fpkl28.png',
    content: `
      <p><strong>The Life and Perfections of Imam Rida (AS)</strong></p>
      <p><strong>By: Shabbir Ahmed Shigri</strong></p>
      <p>An Imam is a being whom Allah has chosen for the guidance of humanity after the Holy Prophet (PBUH). It is necessary that he be pure and infallible from every sin and evil, and, by God's command, be adorned with knowledge of the unseen. Among the pure beings of the family of the Prophet (PBUH), the eighth shining star is the noble personality of Imam Rida (AS). The moral excellence and conduct of Imam Rida (AS) teach us all the art of living. He never offended anyone through his speech. He never interrupted anyone's conversation. He generously helped the needy. He used to eat sitting at the same dining spread with his servants. There was always a smile on his face, but he never laughed loudly. He never sat with his legs stretched out in the presence of others, nor did he lean against a wall. He used to host guests himself. He took great care of the cleanliness and purity of his clothing, hair, and body. On one hand, Imam Ali ibn Musa al-Rida (AS) shared the grief and sorrow of society, and on the other hand, his worship of the Creator of the universe was unparalleled. He used to sleep little at night and spend most of his time in worship. He fasted frequently, and his prostrations were very long. He recited the Holy Quran and strictly observed praying on time. Due to his scholarly perfections, he was given the title "Alim-e Aal-e Muhammad" (The Scholar of the Family of Muhammad). When the renowned scholars of that era and intellectuals of various religions became helpless in scholarly debates, they would turn to none other than Imam Rida (AS). During his journey to Khurasan, he was given a magnificent reception at Nishapur. There, on the insistence of thousands of traditionists and writers, he delivered a great Hadith that reaches from his ancestors to the Prophet (PBUH) and Gabriel (AS), reaching Allah. He said: "The word 'La ilaha illa Allah' is my strong fortress; whoever enters this fortress is safe from My punishment." After this, he said: "However, it has some conditions, and I (meaning the Imam of the time) am one of those conditions." This Hadith is known as the "Hadith of the Golden Chain" (Hadith al-Silsilah al-Dhahab). His noble personality was an embodiment of dignity, seriousness, and gravity. There was neither unnecessary haste nor excessive slowness in his gait. However, when he took a step to help a needy person, there would be speed in it. He used to converse with people freely, but no one ever saw him laughing loudly at joyful events either. His smile was always limited to a gentle one. Due to this dignified personality, no one dared to be impertinent or disrespectful in his gathering. Ali ibn Abi Hamzah al-Bata'ini narrates: One day, a delegation of thirty slaves from Abyssinia arrived in the gathering of Imam Rida (AS). One of the slaves spoke to the Imam (AS) in the Abyssinian language. The Imam (AS) immediately replied in the same local Abyssinian language. I was amazed seeing this scene. The Imam (AS) sensed my astonishment and said: "Are you surprised at my speaking with them in Abyssinian? The station of the Imam is much higher than your thinking. What you saw today is like a bird taking a drop of water from the sea in its beak. Just as this drop does not decrease the water of the sea, similarly, the knowledge of the Imam is also a boundless ocean." Ma'bad ibn Junayd al-Shami narrates that I presented myself in the service of Imam Rida (AS) and submitted: "People talk a lot about your extraordinary miracles. Show me a miracle that I can see myself." The Imam (AS) asked: "What do you want?" I said: "My parents have passed away; resurrect them." The Imam (AS) immediately said: "Go, go to your house; I have brought them back to life." Ma'bad says: By God! When I reached home, my parents were sitting alive. They remained alive for ten days, and then Allah caused them to die again. During the reign of Ma'mun's caliphate, a severe famine occurred in Khurasan, and there was no rain for a long time. Ma'mun requested Imam Rida (AS) to lead the prayer for rain (Salat al-Istisqa'). The Imam (AS) set the condition that people should fast for three days. On Monday, the Imam (AS) went out to the desert with a massive crowd. The Imam (AS) raised his hands towards the sky and prayed: "O Lord! You have made the right of us, the Ahl al-Bayt (AS), obligatory on the people, and they have sought our intercession by Your command. Send down upon them the rain of Your mercy, but give them enough time so that they may reach their homes safely." Within a short time, clouds covered the sky, lightning flashed, and such a torrential downpour occurred that the entire area was drenched. SubhanAllah.</p>
    `
